-- allium: 3
-- pam-audit.allium

-- Scope: the PAM access-audit log — a queryable, governance-grade trail of
-- privileged-access activity (who requested access to what, who decided, who
-- activated it, who ended it), surfaced to org admins.
--
-- This is a WRITTEN, APPEND-ONLY store. Each state-changing PAM action writes an
-- audit event at the moment it happens, through an emit seam
-- (IAccessAuditEventEmitter) that the PAM commands in pam.allium call. A dedicated
-- store backs it (server table [dbo].[AccessAuditEvent]). The governance trail is
-- READ straight from that store — no projection, no joins, no reconstruction from
-- other entities' state. It sits beside pam.allium deliberately: pam.allium models
-- the audit-event EMISSION (the LeaseActivationAudited / LeaseSettles events and
-- the *_at / *_by stamps the commands set); this spec defines the record those
-- emissions write and the views over it.
--
-- Write-ahead (before/after) model: a state-changing action emits a PAIR — an
-- Attempt BEFORE its point of no return and an Outcome AFTER — distinguished by the
-- `phase` field. Emission is non-transactional, so a crash between the two leaves an
-- Attempt with no Outcome (an in-doubt entry) rather than a silently lost event.
--
-- Self-contained rows: the actor / requester / cipher / collection / rule display
-- NAMES are snapshotted into each row at write time (frozen). A later delete or
-- rename of a referenced entity therefore cannot erase or rewrite what an event
-- said, and the read touches no other table. Cipher and collection names are
-- encrypted (captured as-is); IDs are still stored, for correlation.
--
-- One hard constraint shapes the design (external, firm):
--   NOT the generic org event log. The org event feed still records PAM activity
--   in parallel (a separate, standing requirement), but it cannot BE the audit
--   trail: it is a fixed four-column feed, entitlement-gated, and not queryable by
--   collection, requester, approver, verdict or event kind. Its emission — and the
--   fan-out to it — is out of scope here (deferred).
--
-- The written-record principle (the load-bearing idea): the audit is a record
-- CAPTURED AT THE MOMENT OF THE ACTION and SELF-CONTAINED — every descriptive fact
-- (kind, actor, subjects, display names, detail) is snapshotted at write time.
-- Because the record is written rather than reconstructed, repeated actions simply
-- write an event each time, edits and deletes are their own events carrying the
-- snapshotted names, and a refused attempt is written like any other (an Outcome of
-- kind lease_activation_rejected). Each kind in the taxonomy is annotated with what
-- emits it, or with the gap that defers it.
--
-- Scope / still deferred (all firm for this pass):
--   - Dapper / MSSQL only — no EF / self-host store yet.
--   - Org event-log fan-out (the parallel four-column feed) — deferred.
--   - MSSQL Ledger / tamper-evidence — deferred; immutability is a property of the
--     write-once store today (a nice-to-have hardening, not yet enforced).
--   - Retention / export beyond the view window — deferred.
--   - The time-derived kinds (expiry) and the credential-access and
--     kill-switch / freeze kinds — no action emits them yet; see the taxonomy.
--
-- Includes: the audit event record and its taxonomy; what emits each kind; the
--   org-admin audit view and the per-request timeline; the view window.
-- Excludes: generic org-event-log emission and fan-out (parallel requirement,
--   deferred); the deferred event kinds (credential access, expiry sweep,
--   kill-switch / freeze) — see the taxonomy and Emission notes.

use "./pam.allium" as pam

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The audit event record (an entity: written once at action time, never mutated)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- One row in the audit trail: an append-only record written by the emit seam at
-- the moment a PAM action happens. It is an `entity` (not a `value`) because it has
-- identity and durable, standalone existence — a stored row backs it. Rows are
-- written once and never updated (append-only); the taxonomy and the two views are
-- the only readers.
--
-- `kind` carries the outcome (an approval and a denial are distinct kinds), so no
-- separate verdict field is needed. `phase` places the row in the write-ahead pair:
-- an Attempt written before the action's point of no return, an Outcome written
-- after. `actor` is who performed the event — the approver on a decision, the
-- revoker on a revoke, the requester on a submission or self-end; `requester` is
-- the owner of the subject request / lease. Together they give governance the
-- requester-versus-approver distinction. A system-driven event (expiry, an
-- automatic decision) has a null actor.
--
-- The entity references (actor, requester, collection, cipher, request, lease,
-- rule) are retained for CORRELATION. The *_name fields alongside them are the
-- DISPLAY NAMES snapshotted at write time, so a later delete or rename cannot
-- rewrite what the event said; the read never dereferences the entity links.
entity AccessAuditEvent {
    kind: AccessAuditEventKind
    phase: Phase
    occurred_at: Timestamp
    organization: pam/Organization

    -- Who performed it; null for a system / automatic event.
    actor: pam/User?
    -- The owner of the subject request or lease (the access requester).
    requester: pam/User?

    -- Subjects (IDs, for correlation), present according to the event's kind.
    collection: pam/Collection?
    cipher: pam/Cipher?
    request: pam/AccessRequest?
    lease: pam/AccessLease?
    rule: pam/AccessRule?

    -- Display names snapshotted at write time (frozen; the read joins nothing).
    -- actor_name / requester_name are plaintext; cipher_name / collection_name are
    -- encrypted (EncString), captured as-is. Each is present only when its subject
    -- is. See the cipher-name open question for a known future break.
    actor_name: String?
    requester_name: String?
    cipher_name: String?
    collection_name: String?
    rule_name: String?

    -- Whatever free text the source carried: an approver comment, an auto-denial
    -- reason, or a revoke reason. Null when the source had none.
    detail: String?

    -- The lease window, for lease-scoped events (activation, extension, end).
    lease_not_before: Timestamp?
    lease_not_after: Timestamp?

    -- Derived: a system / automatic event has no human actor. Drives the
    -- automated-versus-human filter governance asks for.
    automated: actor = null
}

-- The write-ahead pair. Every state-changing action writes an `attempt` before its
-- point of no return and an `outcome` after; a crash between them leaves an attempt
-- with no matching outcome (an in-doubt entry) rather than a lost event.
enum Phase { attempt | outcome }

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Audit event taxonomy + what emits each kind
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The kinds of audit event, grouped as in the governance design. Each literal
-- notes what emits it, or why it is deferred:
--   (EMITTED)     written today by the command that performs the action, as an
--                 attempt/outcome pair through the emit seam.
--   (DEFERRED)    no action emits it yet — contributes no events; see the note.
enum AccessAuditEventKind {
    -- Access requests --------------------------------------------------------
    -- (EMITTED) by the submit command; requester + subject captured. An
    -- auto-approved request also emits request_approved in the same step.
    request_submitted |
    -- (EMITTED) by the approve command; actor = approver (null = automatic).
    request_approved |
    -- (EMITTED) by the deny command; actor = approver (null = auto-deny),
    -- detail = approver_comment / auto-deny reason. A managing approver's
    -- retraction (ApproverRetractsRequest) emits this too — a denial that names the
    -- approver — distinct from a requester's own cancel below.
    request_denied |
    -- (EMITTED) by the requester's cancel command; actor = requester (approver null).
    request_cancelled |
    -- (DEFERRED) a pending request no approver decided, lapsing at its decision
    -- deadline (PendingRequestExpires). Time-derived: no command performs it, so it
    -- needs a background sweep to emit. None runs yet — contributes no events.
    request_expired_unanswered |
    -- (DEFERRED) an approved request whose activation window lapsed unused
    -- (ApprovedRequestExpiresUnactivated). Time-derived like the above; needs the
    -- same background sweep to emit. None runs yet — contributes no events.
    request_expired_unactivated |

    -- Access leases ----------------------------------------------------------
    -- (EMITTED) by the activation command alongside minting the lease; actor =
    -- requester; lease window captured in lease_not_before / lease_not_after.
    lease_activated |
    -- (EMITTED) by the activation command when a start is refused
    -- (LeaseActivationRejected); actor = requester, detail = the refusal reason.
    -- Each refused attempt is written, so the full history is retained (no longer
    -- a last-only stamp).
    lease_activation_rejected |
    -- (EMITTED) by the extension activation; old end = the extension request's
    -- requested_not_before, new end = requested_not_after.
    lease_extended |
    -- (EMITTED) by the revoke path (SettleLease with a revoker). Covers both an
    -- operator revoke (actor != requester) and a holder self-end (actor =
    -- requester) — pam.allium models both as `revoked`, distinguished by whether the
    -- revoker is the requester.
    lease_revoked |
    -- (DEFERRED) a lease that lapsed on its own at not_after (SettleLease with no
    -- revoker, driven by the LeaseExpires timer). Time-derived: needs the background
    -- sweep to emit. None runs yet — contributes no events.
    lease_expired |

    -- Credential access ------------------------------------------------------
    -- (DEFERRED) opening a leased credential; no command emits it yet.
    credential_accessed |
    -- (DEFERRED) a blocked read (failed re-check); no command emits it yet.
    credential_access_denied |

    -- Rule administration ----------------------------------------------------
    -- (EMITTED) by the create-rule command; rule name snapshotted.
    rule_created |
    -- (EMITTED) by the update-rule command; each edit is its own event carrying the
    -- rule name as it stood at write time (no longer a latest-only projection).
    rule_updated |
    -- (EMITTED) by the delete-rule command as the Outcome; the rule name is
    -- captured before the hard delete, so the row need not survive to name it.
    -- Scoped org-wide with the other rule kinds.
    rule_deleted |

    -- System controls --------------------------------------------------------
    -- (DEFERRED) the org-wide kill switch and leasing freeze; no command emits these
    -- yet. To be revisited with the system-controls surface.
    leasing_kill_switch_triggered |
    leasing_freeze_enabled |
    leasing_freeze_lifted
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Emission notes: how the written model handles the awkward cases
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- A written, self-contained record sidesteps the reconstruction hazards that a
-- query-time projection had to work around. The cases worth naming:
--
--   1. REPEATED actions (a re-activation, repeated rule edits, repeated refusals).
--      Each occurrence writes its own row at the moment it happens, so nothing is
--      collapsed. (credential_accessed / credential_access_denied would follow the
--      same pattern; they are deferred only because no command emits them yet.)
--
--   2. EDITS (rule_updated; a lease extension changing the end). The edit is its own
--      event, capturing the values and the display name as they stood at write time.
--      No prior row is overwritten — the earlier events remain. (Lease extension
--      records old end = the extension request's requested_not_before and new end =
--      requested_not_after, so lease_extended is exact.)
--
--   3. DELETES (rule_deleted; a future leasing-freeze lift). The delete is its own
--      event. The rule is hard-deleted; the rule_deleted Outcome carries the rule
--      name captured by the delete command before the row is removed, so the emit
--      needs nothing to survive; see Supporting change A. The delete cannot rewrite
--      what earlier events said — their rule_name is already snapshotted.
--
--   4. REFUSED attempts (lease_activation_rejected). A refused start is emitted like
--      any other outcome — the request did not transition, but the event is still
--      written (actor = requester, detail = the reason). The refusal is captured
--      solely by this written event.

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Supporting state in pam.allium (landed)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- One change in pam.allium supports the emit path, recorded here as the audit log's
-- prerequisite. It has landed.
--
--   A. (IMPLEMENTED) AccessRule hard-delete + command-supplied name (supports
--      rule_deleted).
--      - OrgAdminDeletesAccessRule clears the rule's collection links and then
--        removes the rule (`not exists rule`) — no deleted_at / deleted_by stamp.
--      - The rule_deleted event's name is supplied by the delete command, captured
--        before the delete, so the audit no longer needs the row to survive to name
--        it (scoped org-wide, like the other rule kinds).

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How far back the audit view surfaces events. Defaults to the window PAM
    -- already uses for request/lease history, so the audit view and the governance
    -- history stay aligned. Retention/export beyond this window is deferred.
    audit_history_window: Duration = pam/config.history_window
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Reads (deferred: the queries over the written store)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- `AccessAuditTrail(viewer, organization, since)`: the stored audit events across
-- the whole `organization` (the viewer holds the AccessEventLogs permission),
-- occurring at or after `since` — a direct read of the AccessAuditEvent store, no
-- projection or joins. Returns Set<AccessAuditEvent>. Deferred because the query is
-- mechanical read-and-filter detail, not domain behaviour; the record and taxonomy
-- above are its contract. The (DEFERRED) kinds contribute no rows until a command
-- or sweep emits them.
deferred AccessAuditTrail    -- see: server-side read of the AccessAuditEvent store

-- `AccessAuditTimeline(request)`: the stored events for a single AccessRequest,
-- oldest first — the request-scoped slice of the same store (its submission,
-- decision, activation, extension, end, and any rejected activation). Returns
-- Sequence<AccessAuditEvent>. Bounded by the request's own lifecycle, so no
-- history window applies.
deferred AccessAuditTimeline    -- see: server-side read of the AccessAuditEvent store

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actors
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Governance audience: anyone who holds the AccessEventLogs permission on the
-- organization (org owners and admins by default, plus any custom role granted it) —
-- the same permission that gates the organization event log. A viewer sees the
-- ENTIRE organization's trail, regardless of which collections they manage: audit
-- visibility is a permission, not a function of collection management.
actor GovernanceViewer {
    identified_by: pam/User
    within: pam/Organization
}

-- The audience for one request's timeline: anyone already authorized to see the
-- request — its requester (via MyAccessRequestsPage), an eligible approver (via
-- ApproverInbox), or an org admin (via the governance trail). The timeline grants
-- no new visibility — identity is the request's existing PAM authorization — so it
-- is captured in prose rather than re-encoded here.
actor AccessRequestViewer {
    identified_by: pam/User
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces: the org-admin audit view, and the per-request timeline
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

surface AccessAuditLog {
    -- The privileged-access audit trail for the whole organization: structured rows
    -- the viewer can filter — the job the generic event feed cannot do. Surfaced as
    -- a section of the governance dashboard — the "what happened" companion to the
    -- dashboard's "what's happening now"; see the related link.
    facing viewer: GovernanceViewer

    context organization: pam/Organization

    exposes:
        -- The stored org-wide trail, within the view window.
        AccessAuditTrail(viewer, organization, now - config.audit_history_window)

    related:
        -- Co-located with the governance dashboard: the audit trail is the
        -- "what happened" tab beside the dashboard's live active/pending state.
        pam/GovernanceDashboard(organization)

    @guarantee ReadOnly
        -- The audit view never mutates PAM state. It reads the AccessAuditEvent
        -- store and provides no actions; writes happen only on the emit path, at the
        -- moment each PAM action occurs.

    @guidance
        -- Filters governance asks for, applied over the returned events: by
        -- collection, cipher, requester, approver (the actor on decision kinds),
        -- event kind, date range, and automated-versus-human (the `automated`
        -- flag) — plus whether a credential was actually accessed, once the
        -- deferred credential_accessed kind lands. Drill-downs: from a request to
        -- its decision timeline (its events ordered by occurred_at), from a cipher
        -- to everyone who held a lease on it, and from a rule to its change history
        -- (each edit is its own event). No CSV / auditor export in this pass
        -- (deferred): the view is read-and-filter only.
        --
        -- Scoping (decided): the trail is org-wide, authorized by the AccessEventLogs
        -- permission — the same gate as the organization event log — not the viewer's
        -- managed collections. Any holder of that permission sees the whole org.
        --
        -- Org suspension: a suspended (disabled) organization drops from the caller's current context, so the
        -- AccessEventLogs permission check fails there and this trail is empty even for an org admin. Shared with the
        -- lease governance reads, not specific to audit; see the pam.allium open question on whether governance review
        -- should survive suspension.
        --
        -- Reach: bounded by audit_history_window, the default view window — not a
        -- retention guarantee. The store keeps rows until retention prunes them;
        -- none is enforced yet (deferred), so it is effectively full history.
        --
        -- In-doubt entries: because emission is non-transactional, an attempt whose
        -- outcome never landed (a crash in between) remains in the store. Governance
        -- may surface these as in-doubt rather than treating them as completed
        -- actions; how they are presented is left to the read.
        --
        -- Persistence: the store is the source of truth. Rows are written once on the
        -- emit path and read back here; the read joins no other table, since every
        -- descriptive fact is snapshotted on the row.
}

surface AccessRequestTimeline {
    -- One request's full story, oldest first: submitted -> decided -> activated ->
    -- extended -> ended, plus any rejected activation. The "what happened to this
    -- request?" drill-down — a read of the stored events for this request.
    facing viewer: AccessRequestViewer

    context request: pam/AccessRequest

    exposes:
        AccessAuditTimeline(request)

    @guarantee ReadOnly
        -- A read of the request's stored events; no actions, no new state.

    @guidance
        -- Reached as a drill-down from ApproverInbox (the history bucket) and
        -- MyAccessRequestsPage (the requester's own rows); an org admin reaches it
        -- from the governance trail. Those inbound links are not modelled as
        -- `related` edges here: they would point pam.allium -> pam-audit, the
        -- reverse of this module's import, and create a cycle. Visibility is
        -- inherited from whichever source surface the viewer arrived through — the
        -- timeline adds none.
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open questions
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- RESOLVED — Audience: org-wide, authorized by the AccessEventLogs permission (the
-- same gate as the organization event log), NOT scoped to the viewer's manageable
-- collections. Audit visibility is a permission, not a function of collection
-- management, so any holder sees the entire organization's trail. (This reverses an
-- earlier decision to scope by manageable collections, which hid rule-administration
-- events whose rule did not yet govern a managed collection.) See the
-- GovernanceViewer actor and AccessAuditTrail(viewer, organization, since).

-- RESOLVED — Retention & export: view-window only for this pass. audit_history_window
-- is the default reach, not a retention SLA, and there is no CSV / auditor export
-- yet (both deferred). The store keeps rows until retention prunes them; none is
-- enforced yet — see the AccessAuditLog reach note.

-- RESOLVED — Deleted rules: a rule delete is a hard delete — the rule is gone, so
-- no active read (gating, GoverningRule, the rules admin list) can find it. Its name
-- lives on in the audit events already written for it, and the delete command
-- supplies the name on the rule_deleted event, so no surviving row is needed. See
-- Supporting change A.

-- KNOWN FUTURE RISK — Cipher-name snapshot: cipher_name is captured at write time
-- today by reading the cipher's server-side name (a JSON_VALUE over the cipher
-- Data). Cipher data is moving to a single opaque encrypted blob with no
-- server-queryable name field; when that lands, this write-time snapshot breaks. The
-- cipher-name handling must then be revisited — store the cipher id only and resolve
-- the name client-side, or have the client supply the encrypted name at emit time.
-- collection_name is not affected (the collection keeps a queryable name).

-- DEFERRED — Org event-log fan-out: the parallel four-column org event feed still
-- records PAM activity as a separate, standing requirement, but emitting to it (and
-- fanning audit events out to it) is out of scope this pass.

-- DEFERRED — Tamper-evidence: the store is append-only and write-once, but
-- immutability is not enforced (no MSSQL Ledger / hash-chaining yet). A hardening to
-- consider when the audit trail must withstand a privileged insider.

-- DEFERRED — Expiry sweep: the time-derived kinds (request_expired_unanswered,
-- request_expired_unactivated, lease_expired) have no command to emit them. A
-- background sweep that emits them when the corresponding timers fire is needed;
-- until it exists these kinds contribute no rows.

-- DEFERRED — Self-host: the store is Dapper / MSSQL only. No EF implementation, so
-- self-hosted deployments have no audit store yet.
